The '''2017 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2016–17 UEFA Europa League]], the 46th season of [[Europe]]'s secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 8th season since it was renamed from the UEFA Cup to the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It was played on 24 May 2017 at the [[Strawberry Arena|Friends Arena]] in [[Solna]], [[Stockholm]], Sweden,<ref name="host">{{cite web |title=Stockholm to host 2017 UEFA Europa League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c510cb9c76a-a7d5bdb2ba3b-1000--stockholm-to-host-2017-uefa-europa-league-final/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 June 2015 |access-date=24 May 2017 }}</ref> between [[Royal Dutch Football Association|Dutch]] side [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] and [[The Football Association|English]] side [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]. Manchester United won the match 2–0 to secure their first title in this competition.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/may/24/manchester-united-ajax-europa-league-final-match-report|title= Manchester United's Paul Pogba sets up Europa League final win over Ajax|date=24 May 2017|work=Guardian|access-date=25 May 2017}}</ref> With this victory, they joined [[Juventus FC|Juventus]], fellow finalists Ajax, [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] and [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] as the only clubs to [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of teams to have won the main European club competitions|won all three major European trophies]] at the time ([[UEFA Champions League|European Champion Clubs' Cup/UEFA Champions League]], UEFA Cup/Europa League, and the now-defunct [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]]);<ref name="main UEFA competitions">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/40004559 |title=Ajax 0 Manchester United 2 |work=BBC Sport |date=24 May 2017 |access-date=25 May 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=20 May 2020 |title=Which teams have won all the major UEFA club trophies? |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025d-0f5b873cb860-61d680b0f8bc-1000--which-teams-have-won-all-the-major-uefa-club-trophies/ |access-date=28 May 2025|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=24 May 2017|title=Manchester United become fifth team to win all three major European trophies |url=https://www.goal.com/en-us/news/manchester-united-become-fifth-team-to-win-all-three-major-european-trophies/1fiu81qn3v8h914r3whq2rd71p |access-date=28 February 2025|website=Goal.com}}</ref> while, with this defeat, Ajax became the fifth club – after [[Hamburger SV]], [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]], [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] – to have lost a final in all these competitions.<ref>{{cite web|first=Roberto |last=Di Maggio |url=https://www.rsssf.org/miscellaneous/intfinalrec.html|title=International Finalists|publisher=RSSSF|date=18 February 2021}}</ref>

Manchester United earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2016–17 UEFA Champions League]], [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], in the [[2017 UEFA Super Cup]]. They also entered the group stage of the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League]], as the berth reserved for the Champions League title holders was not used.<ref>{{cite web |title=Champions League group place on offer for Ajax or Manchester United |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e970bbed2d3-a98a31fc7d8f-1000--europa-league-win-earns-manchester-united-a-champions-league/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=22 May 2017 |access-date=24 May 2017 }}</ref> Police of Stockholm took measures against potential terrorist attacks which they described as "the new normality", since two had occurred in Stockholm in [[2010 Stockholm bombings|2010]] and [[2017 Stockholm truck attack|just a month before the final]], and also prepared for potential [[Football hooliganism|clashes between supporters]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://sverigesradio.se/sida/artikel.aspx?programid=83&artikel=6702579|title=Polisen redo för Europa League-finalen efter dådet i Manchester – Nyheter (Ekot)|language=sv|work=Sveriges Radio|date=23 May 2017 |access-date=15 July 2017 |last1=Hansell |first1=Bengt }}</ref>

It was United's last trophy for nearly six years until they defeated [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] 2–0 to win the [[2023 EFL Cup final]].<ref>{{cite web |last=McNulty |first=Phil |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/64693810|title=Manchester United 2-0 Newcastle United: Erik ten Hag's side win Carabao Cup for first trophy since 2017 |work=BBC Sport|date=26 February 2023|accessdate=26 February 2023 }}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Friends Arena from inside.jpg|thumb|The [[Strawberry Arena|Friends Arena]] in [[Solna]] hosted the final]]
[[Strawberry Arena|Friends Arena]], located in [[Solna Municipality]] of the [[Stockholm urban area]], was announced as the final venue on 30 June 2015, following the decision of the UEFA Executive Committee meeting in [[Prague]], Czech Republic.<ref name="host"/>

==Background==
This was Ajax's second final in the UEFA Cup/Europa League, having won in the [[1992 UEFA Cup Final]] over [[Torino FC|Torino]] on away goals.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0239-0e1680b9e7d1-2917ed41b578-1000--uelfinal-ajax-v-man-united-all-you-need-to-know/|title=#UELfinal, Ajax v Man. United: all you need to know|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=11 May 2017}}</ref>

This was Manchester United's first final in the UEFA Cup/Europa League. They were seeking to become only the fifth club to have [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of teams to have won the main European club competitions|won all three major European trophies]] at the time ([[UEFA Champions League|European Champion Clubs' Cup/UEFA Champions League]], UEFA Cup/Europa League, and the now-defunct [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]]).<ref name="main UEFA competitions"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0251-0e96f862f259-cf0ccb7cf3e4-1000--tottenham-eye-rare-european-clean-sweep/|title=Manchester United aiming for European clean sweep|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=11 May 2017}}</ref>

The two sides had previously met four times in European competitions, all in the UEFA Cup/Europa League, with a record of two wins each. However, Manchester United had eliminated Ajax on both occasions, 2–1 on aggregate in the [[1976–77 UEFA Cup]] first round, and 3–2 on aggregate in the [[2011–12 UEFA Europa League]] round of 32.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0239-0e96fa16b146-14eee51556cc-1000--ajax-v-manchester-united-the-past-meetings/|title=Ajax v Manchester United: the past meetings|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 May 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefaeuropaleague/2017/e/e_2019702_pk.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180507090627/http://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefaeuropaleague/2017/e/e_2019702_pk.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=7 May 2018|title= Match Press Kit|date=24 May 2017|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=24 May 2017}}</ref>

