==Pre-match==

===Ambassador===
The ambassador for the final was former Swedish international player [[Patrik Andersson]], who won the Champions League with [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] against [[Valencia CF|Valencia]] in [[2001 UEFA Champions League Final|2001]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Patrik Andersson the face of Stockholm final|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0230-0e94c11119d3-8d72bfbd4e61-1000--patrik-andersson-the-face-of-stockholm-final/|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=23 August 2016}}</ref>

===Identity===
UEFA unveiled the brand identity of the final on 26 August 2016 in [[Monaco]] during the group stage draw.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0230-0e94d179d9e5-01e9bf3c3f10-1000--stockholm-2017-final-identity-revealed/|title=Stockholm 2017 final identity revealed|website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=26 August 2016}}</ref>

===Ticketing===
With a stadium capacity of 48,000 for the final, a total number of 37,000 tickets were available to fans and the general public, with the two finalist teams receiving 10,000 tickets each and with 17,000 tickets being available for sale to fans worldwide via UEFA.com from 17 to 28 March 2017 in four price categories: €150, €100, €70 and €45. The remaining tickets were allocated to the local organising committee, UEFA and national associations, commercial partners and broadcasters, and to serve the corporate hospitality programme.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2449690.html|title=Tickets for 2017 UEFA Europa League final in Stockholm on sale|website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 March 2017}}{{dead link|date=March 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

===Officials===
In May 2017, Slovenian [[referee (association football)|referee]] [[Damir Skomina]] was chosen to oversee the final. He was joined by compatriots Jure Praprotnik and Robert Vukan as [[assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]], [[Matej Jug]] and [[Slavko Vinčić]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Additional assistant referee|additional assistant referees]], Tomaž Klančnik as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fifth official and reserve assistant referee|reserve assistant referee]], and Italian official [[Gianluca Rocchi]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fourth official|fourth official]].<ref name="referee">{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0239-0e96fa16ab3b-80251dc425b2-1000--damir-skomina-to-referee-europa-league-final/|title=Damir Skomina to referee Europa League final|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=12 May 2017 |access-date=13 May 2017}}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
A minute's silence was observed before the final in memory to victims of the [[Manchester Arena bombing|bombing in Manchester]] which occurred two days before the final; the opening ceremony was thus considerably reduced.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0239-0e97125b21e7-0c68c151261f-1000--minute-s-silence-at-uefa-europa-league-final/|title=Minute's silence at UEFA Europa League final|website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=23 May 2017}}</ref>

==Match==
[[File:2017 UEFA Europa League Final first half.jpg|thumb|The players during the first half of the game.]]

===Summary===
[[Paul Pogba]] opened the scoring for Manchester United in the 18th minute when his low left foot shot from just outside the penalty area took a deflection off [[Davinson Sánchez]] which wrong footed the goalkeeper before looping over him and into the net.<ref>{{cite news |title=Manchester United beat Ajax to claim Europa League title |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c5117a4c425-9136199d2703-1000--manchester-united-beat-ajax-to-claim-europa-league-title/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 May 2017 |access-date=24 May 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2017/05/25/man-utd-win-europa-league-reaction-latest-jose-mourinhos-side/|title= Live Man Utd win Europa League – reaction and latest as Jose Mourinho's side qualify for Champions League|date=24 May 2017|work=The Telegraph|access-date=25 May 2017}}</ref> [[Henrikh Mkhitaryan]] got the second goal three minutes into the second half when with his back to goal he flicked the ball into the net with his right foot from three yards out after the ball had been knocked down to him by [[Chris Smalling]] after a corner from the right by [[Juan Mata]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2017/may/24/ajax-v-manchester-united-europa-league-final-live?page=with:block-5925e6a6e4b00493c8275a66#liveblog-navigation|title= Ajax 0 Manchester United 2: As it happened |date=24 May 2017|work=The Guardian|access-date=25 May 2017}}</ref> [[Wayne Rooney]], who entered minutes before the final whistle, took the captain's armband and later led the team to lift the trophy in his final game for United; he would return to [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] less than two months later.

===Details===
The "home" team (for administrative purposes) was determined by an additional draw held after the semi-final draw, which was held on 21 April 2017 at UEFA headquarters in [[Nyon]], Switzerland.<ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA Europa League semi-final draw |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/draws/2017/2000796/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=3 June 2017}}</ref> Ajax as the "home" team played with their new home kit which would be used the following season. United wore their outgoing away (European third) kit.

